     change to observe.
  2. [1mNotifications[0m — subscription ("care about"), change detection, and delivery. Designed against a real model, once
     one exists.

  We'd brainstorm #1 properly now, and #2 after. Your original ask is still the destination; this is just the order that
  avoids throwaway work.

  [1mThe one thing I need before scoping #1[0m — because it decides what "notifications" even means:

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether more than one person touches the same task. If several people do, a notification is a [3msocial[0m event —
  someone else changed something, and you need telling because you weren't there. That requires identity, ownership,
  and sharing in the foundation, and it makes real delivery (email/push) the point, since the whole value is reaching
  you when you're not looking. If it's just you, nobody else can surprise you, and "notification" almost always turns
  out to mean a [3mreminder[0m — due dates, nudges, "this is overdue" — which is a much smaller feature with no accounts and
  no server strictly required.

  These are expensive to swap later. Retrofitting multi-user onto a single-user model means reworking storage, every
  task record, and the whole auth story. Going the other way is wasted effort rather than rework.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m your phrasing — "tasks [3mthey[0m care about" — reads multi-user to me, so that's my guess. But I'd
  rather you confirm than have me build accounts you never wanted.
